/* ============================================================================
complex-modulus.epher -- the modulus |z| of several complex numbers

|a + bi| = sqrt(a^2 + b^2) is the distance of z from the origin in the
complex plane (any complex analysis text, e.g. Churchill and Brown,
"Complex Variables and Applications", sec. 1). The first two examples are
the Pythagorean triples 3-4-5 and 5-12-13, so their moduli are the exact
integers 5 and 13. The built-in abs gives the modulus; a def restates the
classroom formula sqrt(re^2 + im^2) and is checked against it.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-modulus.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\complex\complex-modulus.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-modulus.epher

No knobs to edit; the values below are fixed examples.
============================================================================ */

// ---- helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------
def modz(z) = sqrt(re(z) ^ 2 + im(z) ^ 2)   // classroom formula; builtin: abs

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("|3 + 4i|:", abs(3 + 4i))
print("|5 - 12i|:", abs(5 - 12i))
print("|1 + i|:", abs(1 + i))
print("|2 - 3i|:", abs(2 - 3i))
print("classroom formula on 3 + 4i:", modz(3 + 4i))
print("formula matches builtin:", abs(modz(3 + 4i) - abs(3 + 4i)) < 1e-12)
/* ---- expected output ----
= |3 + 4i|: 5
= |5 - 12i|: 13
= |1 + i|: 1.41421356237
= |2 - 3i|: 3.60555127546
= classroom formula on 3 + 4i: 5
= formula matches builtin: true
*/
